    "evidence_text": "There was no significant difference in TBS.\n\nPartitioning the symptoms of hypoglycaemia using multi-sample confirmatory factor analysis\n\nSETTING One routine diabetic unit in a university teaching hospital.   \n\n\nHypoglycemic episodes and risk of dementia in older patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus.\n\nWhether minor hypoglycemic episodes increase risk of dementia is unknown.\n\nResults:\n\nDementia risk factors need to be addressed as early as midlife, before underlying disease(s) or symptoms appear.\n\nOBJECTIVE Chronic physical comorbidity is common in dementia.\n\nOnly hearing impairment made a notable independent contribution to cognitive impairment.   \n\n\n4.4 years, mean +/-\n\nThe majority of events were related to missed meals or increased activity.\n\nHowever, in 38% no predisposing factor was evident.   \n\n\nCONCLUSIONS Newly diagnosed children appear to be protected from severe hypoglycemia.\n\nImpaired awareness of hypoglycaemia: a review.\n\nThe development of IAH and counter-regulatory failure greatly increases the risk of severe hypoglycaemia.\n\nAggregation of vascular risk factors and risk of incident Alzheimer disease.\n\nThe presence of vascular risk factors was related to incident possible and probable AD.   \n",
